From 401dfa95b3e946707f1ea988e5cbd8077936cf07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cursor <cursor@example.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 13:29:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(subscription): stop per-frame repaints that make the
 route stutter under the finger
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Подписка route runs two `.hover-border-gradient` elements at once (the
connect-device CTA and the purchase CTA). Their rotation animates
`--border-angle`, a registered custom property feeding a conic-gradient, so
Chromium cannot composite it: every frame repaints the element's whole
background and re-rasters the scroll tiles underneath. Over 6s of synthetic
touch scrolling at 6x CPU throttle that is ~2100ms of main-thread render work
(Paint ~330ms, RasterTask ~1250ms, style recalc ~380ms); freezing the angle on
touch/small screens brings it to ~30ms (0 / 0.2 / 10). The border keeps the
accent sheen it shows in any single frame of the rotation, and desktop keeps
the motion — the same trade the cabinet already makes for backdrop-blur below
1024px. `animate-unlimited-flow` animates `background-position`, also
paint-only, and gets the same treatment.

Second source of hitching: the traffic-refresh (30s) and reissue (15min)
cooldowns were decrementing counters held by the page component, so the whole
2100-line route re-rendered once a second for the entire cooldown. They are
now epoch-ms deadlines, with the per-second tick moved into the memoised leaf
that displays it. Memoising the glass-colour object restores the memo on
<CountdownTimer>, which a fresh object every render had been defeating.
